# Certificate trust for runtimes that do not read the macOS keychain.
#
# privoxy inspects HTTPS and signs with this machine's own CA — see Setup.md,
# "HTTPS inspection, and what it costs". Safari, Chrome, macOS curl, Firefox
# and Zen all follow the keychain and need nothing. Runtimes that ship their
# own trust store do not benefit from that, which is the case the same section
# already calls out. Measured here: npm fails with
# UNABLE_TO_VERIFY_LEAF_SIGNATURE, and opencode — whose runtime is in the same
# family — therefore cannot install the provider package it needs to talk to
# ollama at all.
#
# The bundle is not a new one. It is what the privoxy role already assembles
# for privoxy's own client side: Homebrew's ca-certificates plus this
# machine's keychain roots, so it carries both the LocalKubernetes root and
# the Privoxy HTTPS Inspection intermediate.
#
# Guarded on existence rather than exported blind. It is machine state under
# ~/.local/state, which CONVENTIONS.md §2a defines as safe to delete and
# rewritten by its owner — and Node warns on every start when
# NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS names a file that is not there.
#
# Scope is deliberately Node only. Python has its own knobs
# (REQUESTS_CA_BUNDLE, SSL_CERT_FILE) and is not set here, because nothing
# Python-side needs them any more. aider did, and has been removed in favour
# of goose; azure-cli needed it too, briefly, until the calendar work that
# pulled it in settled on EventKit instead - uv is the only Python left behind
# the proxy, and it takes UV_SYSTEM_CERTS from its own callers.
#
# goose itself needs nothing from this file. It is Rust with a first-class
# ollama provider, so a model call never leaves the loopback and never meets
# an inspected certificate. opencode remains the reason the file exists.
if [[ -r "$HOME/.local/state/privoxy/trusted-cas.pem" ]]; then
  export NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS="$HOME/.local/state/privoxy/trusted-cas.pem"
fi
